<!-- 
Christopher Hitchens's comprehensive argument against censorship,
organized into foundational principles, practical objections,
and applied case studies.
-->


## THESIS

[No Censorship Ever]: Speech should never be censored, 
regardless of content or potential harm.


## PART I: FOUNDATIONAL PRINCIPLES

<!-- 
These establish the positive case for absolute free speech 
based on philosophical and moral grounds.
-->


### A. The Audience's Essential Right

[Audience Right to Hear]: The audience's right to hear is 
as important as the speaker's right to speak. 
#foundational

<Milton Argument>: Milton, Paine, and Mill establish that 
free speech protects listeners as much as speakers. 
#foundational

(1) Milton, Paine, and Mill establish that free speech is 
not just about the speaker.
(2) Every time you silence somebody, you make yourself a 
prisoner of your own action.
(3) You deny yourself the right to hear something.
(4) The right of everyone in the audience to listen is 
equally involved.
----
(5) [Audience Right to Hear]

<Milton Argument>
  -> [Audience Right to Hear]

[Audience Right to Hear]
  -> [No Censorship Ever]


### B. The Special Value of Dissent

[Heretic Value]: Dissenting minority views are especially 
valuable and must be protected. #foundational

<Mill Heretic Argument>: John Stuart Mill argued that the 
lone dissenter is more important precisely because of the 
consensus. #foundational

(1) If all society agreed on one proposition except one 
person, that one heretic should be heard.
(2) It becomes more important to hear the heretic precisely 
because of the consensus.
(3) We benefit from even outrageous or appalling views.
(4) Rosa Luxemburg said freedom of speech is meaningless 
unless it means the freedom of the person who thinks 
differently.
----
(5) [Heretic Value]

<Mill Heretic Argument>
  -> [Heretic Value]

[Heretic Value]
  -> [No Censorship Ever]


<Holocaust Denier Example>: Even Holocaust denial must be 
protected to maintain free speech principles. #foundational

(1) If everyone is taught one official story of the 
Holocaust and one person denies it happened, that person's 
right to speak must be given extra protection.
(2) What they say took effort to formulate and might 
contain a grain of historical truth however unlikely.
(3) It makes people question how do I know what I think I 
know.
----
(4) Even the most offensive speech deserves protection.

<Holocaust Denier Example>
  -> [Heretic Value]


### C. The Self-Protection Principle

[Censorship Threatens Censor]: Every act of censorship 
creates precedent that can be used against the censor. 
#foundational

<Man For All Seasons>: Thomas More's warning about cutting 
down legal protections. #foundational

(1) Thomas More argued with a witch hunting prosecutor who 
said I would cut down every law in England to capture the 
devil.
(2) More replied when the devil turned to meet you where 
would you run for protection.
(3) All the laws having been cut down and flattened who 
would protect you then.
----
(4) [Censorship Threatens Censor]

<Man For All Seasons>
  -> [Censorship Threatens Censor]

[Censorship Threatens Censor]
  -> [No Censorship Ever]


## PART II: PRACTICAL IMPOSSIBILITIES

<!-- 
These demonstrate that censorship cannot work 
even if we wanted it to.
-->


### A. The Authority Problem

[No Trusted Censor]: No person or institution can be 
trusted with the authority to determine what speech should 
be censored. #practical

<Authority Argument>: When asked who should decide what we 
can read or hear, no one volunteers. #practical

(1) Censorship requires someone to decide what speech is 
harmful.
(2) This decision maker must be trusted with authority over 
others access to ideas.
(3) To whom would you give this job? Do you know anyone 
worthy of deciding what you can read or hear?
(4) No one in the audience raised their hand to nominate 
someone.
----
(5) [No Trusted Censor]

<Authority Argument>
  -> [No Trusted Censor]

[No Trusted Censor]
  -> [No Censorship Ever]


#### Case Study 1: The Censors Seek What They Suppress

<Dr Johnson Argument>: Dr. Johnson caught respectable 
ladies searching his dictionary for the indecent words they 
claimed to oppose. #practical

(1) Dr Johnson compiled the first great English dictionary.
(2) Respectable ladies congratulated him for excluding 
indecent words.
(3) Dr Johnson replied Ladies I congratulate you on being 
able to look them up.
(4) The ladies had searched through the dictionary 
specifically looking for filthy words.
----
(5) Those who seek to censor are often seeking the content 
they claim to suppress.
(6) One has to suspect the motives of those who are 
determined to be offended.

<Dr Johnson Argument>
  -> [No Trusted Censor]


#### Case Study 2: The Wise Judge Makes Foolish Decisions

<Holmes Misuse Argument>: Oliver Wendell Holmes misused 
his fire in a crowded theater standard to jail peaceful 
socialists. #practical

(1) Oliver Wendell Holmes created the fire in a crowded 
theater standard.
(2) He used this to jail Yiddish speaking socialists who 
opposed Wilson's entry into WWI in a language most 
Americans could not read.
(3) They were refugees from the war they were trying to 
prevent.
(4) The socialists could be seen as the real firefighters 
warning of genuine danger.
----
(5) The supposedly wise Holmes misused censorship power.
(6) Who is to decide who is really shouting fire when there 
is real fire.

<Holmes Misuse Argument>
  -> [No Trusted Censor]


#### Case Study 3: The Wrong Country Judges History

<David Irving Defense>: Austria, which gave the world its 
worst, presumes to judge historical truth. #practical

(1) David Irving is a British historian imprisoned in 
Austria not for what he said but for potentially planning 
to say something.
(2) Austria gave us Kurt Waldheim and Jorg Haider and 
presumes to judge historical truth.
(3) Austria convinced the world Hitler was German and 
Beethoven was Viennese.
----
(4) Even in the hardest case of Holocaust denial censorship 
is wrong.

<David Irving Defense>
  -> [No Trusted Censor]


### B. The Knowledge Problem

[Unpredictable Harm]: It is impossible to know in advance 
what speech will cause harm. #practical

<Epistemology Argument>: Harm from speech depends entirely 
on unpredictable contextual factors. #practical

(1) Harm from speech depends on timing location audience 
circumstances and tone.
(2) These contextual factors cannot be known or predicted 
in advance.
(3) Prior restraint requires knowing in advance what will 
cause harm.
(4) In the US prior restraint is banned by the First 
Amendment.
(5) No one has the knowledge that would be required to make 
that call.
----
(6) [Unpredictable Harm]

<Epistemology Argument>
  -> [Unpredictable Harm]

[Unpredictable Harm]
  -> [No Censorship Ever]


<Fire Theater Context>: The fire in a crowded theater 
example proves harm depends on context. #practical

(1) Who decides who is really warning of fire versus 
falsely alarming.
(2) The Yiddish socialists could claim they were warning of 
real danger.
(3) The context determines whether fire is warning or panic 
inducing.
----
(4) Harm from speech depends entirely on context which 
cannot be predetermined.

<Fire Theater Context>
  -> [Unpredictable Harm]


## PART III: THE RELIGION PARADOX

<!-- 
This reveals the fatal contradiction in hate speech laws: 
the main source of hatred seeks protection from censorship.
-->


### A. The Core Paradox

[Religion Main Hate Source]: Religion and organized 
religion are the main sources of hatred in the world. 
#religious

[Religion Seeks Protection]: Religious groups seek hate 
speech protection while promoting hatred themselves. 
#religious

<Religion Paradox>: The main source of hatred seeks 
protection from hate speech laws. #religious

(1) [Religion Main Hate Source]
(2) How will censorship advocates ban religion or stop 
expression of religious loathing hatred and bigotry.
(3) They cannot because religion is protected expression.
----
(4) [Religion Seeks Protection]
(5) The main source of hatred seeks protection from 
censorship.
(6) This creates a fundamental paradox for hate speech 
laws.

<Religion Paradox>
  -> [No Censorship Ever]


### B. Evidence: Religious Texts as Primary Sources of Hatred

#### Religion and Homophobia

<Bible Incitement>: The Bible explicitly commands hatred of 
homosexuals. #religious

(1) The Bible says you have to hate homosexuals.
(2) If Falwell says this because the Bible says so he is 
right about what the Bible says.
(3) This might make people use violence.
(4) What are you going to do about that.
(5) Religious people will say do not put your hands on our 
Bible or we will call the hate speech police.
----
(6) Religious texts incite hatred and violence.
(7) You cannot ban religion.

<Bible Incitement>
  -> [Religion Main Hate Source]


#### Religion and Anti-Semitism

<Christianity Anti-Semitism>: Christianity provided the 
warrant for two thousand years of Jewish persecution. 
#religious

(1) Kristallnacht was not the result of 10 years of Jew 
baiting but the result of 2000 years of Christianity.
(2) Based on one verse of St John's Gospel claiming Jews 
demanded Christ's blood on themselves and all their 
children.
(3) This led to pogroms after every Easter sermon for 
hundreds of years.
----
(4) This is the warrant and license for anti Jewish 
pogroms.
(5) What are you going to do about that.
(6) You cannot censor St John's Gospel.

<Christianity Anti-Semitism>
  -> [Religion Main Hate Source]


#### Religion as Universal Warrant for Oppression

<Warrant for Oppression>: Religious texts provide the 
warrant for every form of hatred and oppression. #religious

(1) Look for the warrant for slavery subjection of women 
burning homosexuals ethnic cleansing anti-Semitism.
(2) You look no further than a famous book that is on every 
pulpit in every synagogue and in every mosque.
----
(3) Religious texts incite hatred and oppression.
(4) The force that is the main source of hatred is also the 
main caller for censorship.

<Warrant for Oppression>
  -> [Religion Main Hate Source]
  -> [Religion Seeks Protection]


### C. The Evolutionary Permanence of Religion

<Evolution of Religion>: Religious belief is permanent due 
to our evolutionary limitations. #religious

(1) Religious belief is ineradicable as long as we remain 
stupid poorly evolved mammals.
(2) Our prefrontal lobes are too small and our adrenaline 
glands are too big.
(3) We are afraid of the dark and afraid to die.
(4) We believe truths of holy books that are so stupid a 
child can see through them.
(5) No Canadian law will solve this evolutionary problem.
----
(6) Religion should be treated with ridicule and hatred and 
contempt.
(7) And I claim that right.

<Evolution of Religion>
  -> [No Censorship Ever]


## PART IV: ISLAM AS CONTEMPORARY CASE STUDY

<!-- 
This section applies the general arguments to the most 
pressing contemporary example.
-->


### A. Islam's Unique Position

[Islam Special Threat]: Islam currently poses a serious 
menace to freedoms beyond just speech. #islam

[Islam Dual Position]: Islam is globally powerful but poses 
as a vulnerable minority in Western societies. #islam

<Islam Irony>: The religion that most threatens speech 
freedom seeks the most speech protection. #islam

(1) Not all monotheisms are the same at the moment.
(2) [Islam Special Threat]
(3) Globally Islam is powerful controls oil wealth and 
large countries.
(4) It pumps Wahhabism worldwide poisoning societies 
ruining minds of children training people in violence 
making a cult of death and suicide and murder.
(5) [Islam Dual Position]
(6) In Western societies Islam poses as a cringing minority 
deserving protection.
----
(7) The religion that most threatens speech freedom seeks 
the most speech protection.
(8) This is the ultimate irony of hate speech laws.

<Islam Irony>
  -> [Religion Seeks Protection]


### B. The Right to Criticize Religious Claims

<Prophet Criticism>: Muhammad's revelation claims are 
unlikely and deserve criticism. #islam

(1) Islam claims final revelation through an archangel to 
one illiterate businessman.
(2) The resulting material is largely plagiarized from the 
Old and New Testament.
(3) It should be accepted as divine revelation and final 
and unalterable.
(4) Those who do not accept are fit to be treated as cattle 
infidels slaves and victims.
(5) I do not think Muhammad ever heard those voices.
(6) I am more likely objectively correct than the 
traditional account.
----
(7) But who is the one under threat.
(8) The person who promotes unverifiable claims gets 
protection.
(9) The person who says this is silly enough to cartoon 
faces threats.

<Prophet Criticism>
  -> [No Censorship Ever]


### C. The Cartoon Controversy: Inverting Protection

<Cartoon Controversy>: Hate speech laws protect those who 
threaten violence while punishing critics. #islam

(1) I say Islam is silly enough to cartoon.
(2) Response is placards saying behead those who cartoon 
Islam in London Toronto New York.
(3) These threateners do not get arrested for hate speech.
(4) I might get in trouble for criticizing Muhammad.
----
(5) Where are your priorities.
(6) You are giving away what is most precious without a 
fight.
(7) You are praising people who want to deny you the right 
to resist.

<Cartoon Controversy>
  -> <Islam Irony>
  -> [No Censorship Ever]


## CONCLUSION: THE FALSE CHOICE

<False Antithesis>: Hate speech laws create a false choice 
that protects hatred while suppressing criticism. #religious

(1) The main source of hatred is religion.
(2) Religion is also the main caller for censorship.
(3) Hate speech laws would protect the primary sources of 
hatred.
(4) You cannot ban religious speech so laws will be 
selectively enforced.
(5) Those who threaten violence seek hate speech protection 
for their views.
----
(6) You are faced with a gigantic false antithesis.
(7) The question is not free speech versus protection from 
hate.
(8) It is free speech versus protecting hatred while 
suppressing criticism of it.

<False Antithesis>
  -> [No Censorship Ever]
